1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:24,640
On October 15, 1959, the Dresden Nuclear Power Station of the Commonwealth Edison Company

2
00:00:24,640 --> 00:00:29,640
sustained for the first time the atomic fission which would be Dresden's source of energy

3
00:00:29,640 --> 00:00:31,320
for the generation of electric power.

4
00:00:37,120 --> 00:00:42,600
This marked the beginning of proving the first full-scale boiling water reactor power station.

5
00:00:44,380 --> 00:00:50,140
Now, accurate information could be compiled which would predict the path nuclear power must take

6
00:00:50,140 --> 00:00:56,160
to provide electricity economically and to determine the procedures of nuclear power station operation.

7
00:01:00,320 --> 00:01:05,659
In April of 1960, 64 tons of slightly enriched uranium oxide

8
00:01:05,659 --> 00:01:09,760
started to do the work of 1 1⁄2 to 2 million tons of coal.

9
00:01:10,700 --> 00:01:12,900
Dresden produced its first electric power.

10
00:01:12,900 --> 00:01:27,280
In December of 1963, after four years of regular operation as a major plant of the

11
00:01:27,280 --> 00:01:31,800
Commonwealth Edison System, responding to the varying load demands day after day,

12
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:36,180
the net electric output passed the 3 billion kilowatt hour mark.

13
00:01:36,180 --> 00:01:47,340
typical load pick up is 1.75 megawatts per minute at low power levels and up to 10 megawatts per

14
00:01:47,340 --> 00:01:57,440
minute at levels above 50% of rated power the station's capacity has been upgraded to 210,000

15
00:01:57,440 --> 00:02:04,680
kilowatts today Dresden's overall performance record is conclusive evidence of its success

16
00:02:04,680 --> 00:02:12,680
Under standard electric utility operating conditions, this nuclear power station is performing with uniform excellence.

17
00:02:16,680 --> 00:02:22,680
The simplicity of the boiling water reactor contributes to the station's high degree of availability to the utility system.

18
00:02:24,680 --> 00:02:28,680
Dresden was designed for thirteen percent average steam void volume.

19
00:02:29,680 --> 00:02:33,680
Experimental tests demonstrated voids as high as thirty percent.

20
00:02:34,680 --> 00:02:40,520
This experience has led to designs for subsequent BWR cores at 28% voids.

21
00:02:46,040 --> 00:02:53,819
In 1962, Dresden was available for the production of electric power 79.4% of the time

22
00:02:53,819 --> 00:03:01,620
and 93.1% of the time over the 11-month period prior to plant shutdown on November 7, 1962.

23
00:03:04,680 --> 00:03:10,120
Based on refueling and modification work during that shutdown,

24
00:03:10,800 --> 00:03:15,000
availabilities of over 90% can be expected for boiling water reactor plants.

25
00:03:17,640 --> 00:03:23,140
During 1962, the plant operated at an average of 73% of its capacity.

26
00:03:24,920 --> 00:03:29,580
During the 11 months prior to plant shutdown, on November 7, 1962,

27
00:03:30,260 --> 00:03:34,000
the capacity factor averaged 85.6%.

28
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:43,759
In comparison, the average availability of six large coal-fired plants on the same line

29
00:03:43,759 --> 00:03:54,800
was 82.4%, with a capacity factor of 67% for the same period.

30
00:03:54,800 --> 00:04:04,480
The capacity factor from start of commercial service to January 1, 1964 was about 50%.

31
00:04:04,480 --> 00:04:10,719
Three major plant shutdowns have been experienced, one for blade and drive modification, one

32
00:04:10,719 --> 00:04:21,240
for a required drive inspection, and the third for turbine and plant modification and refueling.

33
00:04:21,240 --> 00:04:28,139
The refueling of 40% of Dresden's core required 33 days.

34
00:04:28,139 --> 00:04:33,519
More routine refueling calls for replacement of 20% of the core.

35
00:04:33,519 --> 00:04:38,139
As a direct result of this experience, boiling water reactor plants are being designed with

36
00:04:38,139 --> 00:04:42,199
15-day refueling periods.

37
00:04:42,199 --> 00:04:47,879
Zirconium clad fuel exhibited high dependability at Dresden.

38
00:04:47,879 --> 00:04:50,879
Not a single shutdown was due to fuel failure.

39
00:04:50,879 --> 00:04:58,759
Out of 84,000 fuel segments, only five failed, with resulting off-gas activity one one-thousandth

40
00:04:58,759 --> 00:05:05,680
of the license for continuous operation.

41
00:05:05,680 --> 00:05:13,879
Average fuel exposure in Dresden Fuel Cycle 1 was 5,000 megawatt days per ton.

42
00:05:13,879 --> 00:05:23,019
exposure of the bundles removed was 6,400 megawatt days per ton. The disposal of radioactive

43
00:05:23,019 --> 00:05:29,480
waste presents no problem. Stack emission of radio gases averages 0.5 milirems per year

44
00:05:29,480 --> 00:05:39,600
at the point of maximum exposure, about one 300th of the inescapable radiation from nature.

45
00:05:39,600 --> 00:05:43,699
Measurements of water samples taken up and downstream of the plant indicate no measurable

46
00:05:43,699 --> 00:05:50,740
increase in radioactivity due to plant operation. The reactor's bottom-mounted control rods and

47
00:05:50,740 --> 00:05:55,779
drives, which shape the neutron flux for efficient fuel burn-up, operate extremely well.

48
00:05:58,500 --> 00:06:02,899
These drives quickly shut down the reactor under potential emergency conditions.

49
00:06:04,819 --> 00:06:08,819
Not one control rod drive has ever failed to operate when called on to scram.

50
00:06:08,819 --> 00:06:17,519
Boarding water reactor plant operation is safe and easily controllable. The response to controls

51
00:06:17,519 --> 00:06:24,839
is immediate and precise. Here startup and shutdown have been performed with less difficulty

52
00:06:24,839 --> 00:06:31,920
and with greater safety than the starting and stopping of a coal-fired boiler. Plant operators

53
00:06:31,920 --> 00:06:37,620
praise the plants cleanliness and ease of winter operation with freedom from problems of handling

54
00:06:37,620 --> 00:06:44,699
frozen coal. Dresden has also exhibited excellent load-following characteristics

55
00:06:44,699 --> 00:06:53,199
under abnormal conditions. In April of 1962 a severe lightning storm knocked

56
00:06:53,199 --> 00:06:59,240
out a transformer that fed two of the four reactor recirculating pumps.

57
00:07:07,620 --> 00:07:14,620
Reactor power decreased automatically, almost to half of rated power.

58
00:07:14,620 --> 00:07:18,620
The turbine generator followed.

59
00:07:18,620 --> 00:07:26,620
Within nine minutes, the operator supplied auxiliary power to the pumps and returned them to service.

60
00:07:26,620 --> 00:07:30,620
This was the only operator action needed or taken.

61
00:07:30,620 --> 00:07:35,620
Quickly, the reactor and turbine generator recovered to full power.

62
00:07:37,620 --> 00:07:49,860
conditions for contact maintenance at Dresden have been more than satisfactory even during

63
00:07:49,860 --> 00:07:56,899
refueling and modification plant equipment could be inspected in a routine manner inspection of

64
00:07:56,899 --> 00:08:01,860
the turbine revealed the entire unit to be an excellent condition with only light erosion

65
00:08:01,860 --> 00:08:03,860
and corrosion on the internal parts.

66
00:08:08,860 --> 00:08:11,860
The small amount of radiation that exists during operation

67
00:08:11,860 --> 00:08:15,860
due to isotopes of oxygen and nitrogen is short-lived.

68
00:08:20,860 --> 00:08:25,860
Working under practically normal conditions, maintenance crews experienced only 2 to 3 millirems

69
00:08:25,860 --> 00:08:28,860
exposure during a 10-hour shift.

70
00:08:28,860 --> 00:08:34,899
The experience and know-how gained from the operation of this first full-scale boiling

71
00:08:34,899 --> 00:08:42,620
water reactor is helping utilities design and build more efficient power plants.

72
00:08:42,620 --> 00:08:48,919
In its day-to-day operation, Dresden continues to demonstrate the dependability, safety,

73
00:08:48,919 --> 00:08:53,060
and ease of operation and maintenance of the nuclear power station.

74
00:08:53,060 --> 00:08:57,759
Dresden, one giant step forward in the use of nuclear energy.
